WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.540 --> 00:00:02.819
So in this next section,

2
00:00:02.819 --> 00:00:04.480
we're gonna talk about tracking solutions

3
00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:06.980
and the creation of a rigid body objects.

4
00:00:06.980 --> 00:00:08.380
This is required for tracking.

5
00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:09.400
So as we go through here,

6
00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:11.080
we are gonna talk specifically

7
00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:13.019
about the OptiTrack Motive System,

8
00:00:13.019 --> 00:00:14.460
but I'm not gonna demonstrate this

9
00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:16.940
because there is plenty of documentation

10
00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:19.879
on the respective websites for the actual vendor

11
00:00:19.879 --> 00:00:22.339
that provides these tracking solutions.

12
00:00:22.339 --> 00:00:23.260
So I just wanna point out here

13
00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:25.300
that there are active and passive options

14
00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:26.579
that are available out here.

15
00:00:26.579 --> 00:00:30.579
So the 209 group, we use the mine at the latte stage.

16
00:00:30.579 --> 00:00:32.299
It is an active camera tracking unit.

17
00:00:32.299 --> 00:00:34.700
You can just visit the 209 group to purchase this,

18
00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:37.380
although there's probably going to be some of a wait time

19
00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:39.500
for this particular tool set.

20
00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:42.000
There is also the option of just using passive markers

21
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:43.500
and creating your own Sputnik,

22
00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:45.340
much like the Epic Games custom Sputnik

23
00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:47.039
that was created by Kevin Cushing.

24
00:00:47.039 --> 00:00:48.200
This system is passive,

25
00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:50.640
so it doesn't have the battery requirements,

26
00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:52.340
but again, because it's not an active system,

27
00:00:52.340 --> 00:00:53.979
it's not quite as accurate

28
00:00:53.979 --> 00:00:57.200
or can be not as accurate as the active tracking system.

29
00:00:57.200 --> 00:00:59.020
However, because it doesn't require batteries

30
00:00:59.060 --> 00:01:01.020
and it doesn't require any kind of power supply,

31
00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:04.620
it can last much longer than our active systems.

32
00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:07.140
Now, we also have the option of using an OptiTrack Puck

33
00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:09.459
as a tracking unit or the VIVE tracker.

34
00:01:09.459 --> 00:01:11.980
Vicon also has several options as well

35
00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:13.319
for passive marker systems.

36
00:01:13.319 --> 00:01:14.980
If you're using a custom Sputnik,

37
00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:17.299
that would work in both the Vicon

38
00:01:17.299 --> 00:01:19.519
and the OptiTrack systems

39
00:01:19.519 --> 00:01:20.819
because it is a passive system

40
00:01:20.819 --> 00:01:22.739
and markers are typically universal.

41
00:01:22.739 --> 00:01:25.200
So the VIVE tracker, the VIVE Mars tracking system,

42
00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:26.480
those will also work that as well.

43
00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:28.700
Now, any tracked object and CG model

44
00:01:29.299 --> 00:01:30.700
can be created to suit your needs.

45
00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:33.140
The process includes adding calibration points

46
00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:35.019
that are set up correctly on the CG model

47
00:01:35.019 --> 00:01:36.519
to match the tracked object

48
00:01:36.519 --> 00:01:39.659
and that the Latte Stage and Lab use an OptiTrack Puck

49
00:01:39.659 --> 00:01:41.379
for nodal point calibration.

50
00:01:41.379 --> 00:01:43.219
The checkerboard is not required

51
00:01:43.219 --> 00:01:44.540
for nodal point calibration.

52
00:01:44.540 --> 00:01:46.599
So the checkerboard we created in the last class

53
00:01:46.599 --> 00:01:49.500
is not necessary for nodal point calibration,

54
00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:52.659
but it is required for your lens distortion mapping.

55
00:01:52.659 --> 00:01:54.099
Now, the Latte Stage, as I mentioned,

56
00:01:54.099 --> 00:01:56.180
does use the OptiTrack Motive system

57
00:01:56.180 --> 00:01:57.939
and we will provide anyone

58
00:01:57.980 --> 00:01:59.939
who is using the OptiTrack system

59
00:01:59.939 --> 00:02:03.879
with the CG model that we created for the OptiTrack Puck.

60
00:02:03.879 --> 00:02:04.939
It has a little bump,

61
00:02:04.939 --> 00:02:07.879
so it's not exactly matching the OptiTrack Puck,

62
00:02:07.879 --> 00:02:10.340
but that little bump is just the front of the Puck

63
00:02:10.340 --> 00:02:12.259
versus the back of the Puck.

64
00:02:12.259 --> 00:02:14.740
Now, as I go through here and look at my nodal point,

65
00:02:14.740 --> 00:02:16.219
again, we are going to provide you

66
00:02:16.219 --> 00:02:19.580
with the OBJ and FBX that you can use in here.

67
00:02:19.580 --> 00:02:21.379
You can actually bring this into any system.

68
00:02:21.379 --> 00:02:24.099
So if you have a tracked object in the Motive system

69
00:02:24.099 --> 00:02:27.180
or in the Shogun system, you can use this in both.

70
00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:29.740
It is just an FBX, it is just an OBJ.

71
00:02:29.740 --> 00:02:32.300
You have these, this mesh was created in Autodesk Maya.

72
00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:34.340
However, this is not gonna match

73
00:02:34.340 --> 00:02:37.139
Vicon's tracking Puck or their active tracker.

74
00:02:37.139 --> 00:02:39.259
That system is gonna look a little bit different

75
00:02:39.259 --> 00:02:41.669
than what you have here.

76
00:02:41.669 --> 00:02:43.409
Now, the nodal point of a physical camera

77
00:02:43.409 --> 00:02:45.969
is the point where light rays converge in a lens.

78
00:02:45.969 --> 00:02:47.909
And in order to calculate the nodal offset

79
00:02:47.909 --> 00:02:49.490
that will be applied to the cine camera,

80
00:02:49.490 --> 00:02:51.889
a series of tracked objects need to be created

81
00:02:51.889 --> 00:02:54.069
that match the physically tracked object

82
00:02:54.069 --> 00:02:55.909
that is in use by the tracking solution,

83
00:02:55.909 --> 00:02:57.830
such as OptiTrax Motive.

84
00:02:57.830 --> 00:03:00.310
Now, again, we start with our CG model here.

85
00:03:00.310 --> 00:03:03.150
This OptiPuck track object is what's going to go

86
00:03:03.150 --> 00:03:05.069
into two different locations.

87
00:03:05.069 --> 00:03:08.949
So the OBJ version is gonna go into OptiTrax Motive,

88
00:03:08.949 --> 00:03:11.030
and that's what we're gonna use to create a rigid body.

89
00:03:11.030 --> 00:03:13.990
And then the FBX version is gonna go into Unreal Engine.

90
00:03:13.990 --> 00:03:15.870
And we'll talk about actually putting that together

91
00:03:15.870 --> 00:03:16.789
in Unreal Engine.

92
00:03:16.789 --> 00:03:19.430
However, the nodal object that we're going to be

93
00:03:19.430 --> 00:03:22.310
creating for the tracked object in OptiTrax Motive,

94
00:03:22.310 --> 00:03:23.509
we're not actually gonna go through.

95
00:03:23.550 --> 00:03:26.389
Creating a tracked object is something that you can look up

96
00:03:26.389 --> 00:03:30.030
on OptiTrax website and follow their documentation as well.

97
00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:31.849
But we are going to briefly discuss this

98
00:03:31.849 --> 00:03:33.469
by taking a look at the slides here,

99
00:03:33.469 --> 00:03:35.789
which walks you through some steps inside Motive

100
00:03:35.789 --> 00:03:38.229
to select your track markers from your puck itself,

101
00:03:38.229 --> 00:03:40.270
create your tracked object, and then make adjustments.

102
00:03:40.270 --> 00:03:43.229
Now, additionally, you are going to need to tweak

103
00:03:43.229 --> 00:03:46.229
the position of the puck so it is lined up correctly

104
00:03:46.229 --> 00:03:50.389
so that the alignment for the OBJ does match

105
00:03:50.389 --> 00:03:52.330
as close as possible to the markers

106
00:03:52.330 --> 00:03:53.169
that are streaming in here,

107
00:03:53.169 --> 00:03:55.449
because we wanna be able to match and line up

108
00:03:55.449 --> 00:03:58.090
the dimensions of the physical object in CG model

109
00:03:58.090 --> 00:03:59.949
to our tracked object.

110
00:03:59.949 --> 00:04:02.129
So that's it for creating your tracked objects.

111
00:04:02.129 --> 00:04:04.849
Next, we're gonna talk about creating our tracked object

112
00:04:04.849 --> 00:04:05.689
in Unreal Engine.

113
00:04:05.689 --> 00:04:08.449
And again, we are using the OptiTrax Motive system

114
00:04:08.449 --> 00:04:11.289
and the two objects that we have here available to us.
